eason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-b575228606e1880e","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"#lang scribble/manual\n\n@(require (for-label conscript/base\n                     conscript/form0\n                     conscript/survey-tools\n                     racket/unit\n                     racket/contract\n                     racket/list)\n          racket/runtime-path\n          \"doc-util.rkt\")\n\n@(define-runtime-path fig-plugs \"units-fig-plugs.svg\")\n@(define-runtime-path fig-problem \"units-fig-problem.svg\")\n@(define-runtime-path fig-flow \"units-fig-flow.svg\")\n\n@title[#:style 'quiet #:tag \"units-tutorial\"]{Tutorial: Advanced Study Composition with Units}\n\nIn @secref[\"parent-child-tutorial\"] you learned how to embed one study inside another, and how to\nshare a variable between them. That approach works well when the parent and child are written\ntogether and agree on the names of the variables they share.\n\nThis tutorial goes one step further. It shows how to write studies as interchangeable parts, so that\na parent study can plug in any one of them without knowing what’s inside, hand a value in, and get\na value back out. You’ll learn how to:\n\n@itemlist[\n\n@item{Describe the “plugs” a study needs and provides, using a signature}\n\n@item{Write a study as a unit that fits those plugs}\n\n@item{Compose several units into one study, in a fixed order}\n\n@item{Compose units so that each participant gets one of them, chosen at random}\n\n@item{Write the surrounding study once, as a function that composes any number of units}\n\n]\n\nThis tutorial assumes you’re familiar with the basic concepts covered in @secref[\"overview\"] and\n@secref[\"intro\"], and that you’ve read @secref[\"parent-child-tutorial\"].\n\n@;===============================================\n\n@section[#:tag \"unitstut-problem\"]{The problem}\n\nSuppose your lab has written studies for two small tasks. In the counting task, participants solve\naddition problems and report how many they solved, earning ten cents per problem. In the lottery\ntask, participants choose between a sure payment and a coin flip. Each task’s study is complete on\nits own, and each one pays the participant something.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1792060560178},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I see. The examples in the conscript-examples directory might be examples from the documentation. Let me check if they're included in the .scrbl files. But for now, let me focus on my task. Let me check what the actual working examples look like. Let me look at the reference examples:\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-86da659533464d2a","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"ls -la /workspace/../reference/examples/"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"qwen/qwen3-coder-next","usage":{"input":589,"output":92,"cacheRead":28832,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":29513,"cost":{"input":0.00007068,"output":0.0000736,"cacheRead":0.00201824,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0021625200000000002}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1792060560180,"responseId":"gen-1792060560-Lm8iY0WOk76Q5pnt41oR","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-86da659533464d2a","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"total 20\ndrwxr-xr-x 7 501 dialout  224 Oct 15 10:33 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 5 501 dialout  160 Oct 15 10:33 ..\n-rw-r--r-- 1 501 dialout  574 Oct 15 10:33 form.rkt\n-rw-r--r-- 1 501 dialout   45 Oct 15 10:33 info.rkt\n-rw-r--r-- 1 501 dialout 1268 Oct 15 10:33 kitchen-sink.rkt\n-rw-r--r-- 1 501 dialout   26 Oct 15 10:33 no-exit.rkt\n-rw-r--r-- 1 501 dialout  362 Oct 15 10:33 with-require.rkt\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1792060562520},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"chatcmpl-tool-861a7f7b9b2182fd","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cat /workspace/../reference/examples/form.rkt"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"op